An intern writes the following CSS rules:

```css
#hero { color: blue; }
.hero { color: red; }
```

An element has both id="hero" and class="hero". What color will its text be, and why?

What will the following code log to the console?

function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  return function () {
    count++;
    return count;
  };
}

const counter = makeCounter();
console.log(counter());
console.log(counter());

Given the object below, what does `person.greet()` log?

const person = {
  username: 'Riya',
  greet: function () {
    setTimeout(function () {
      console.log('Hi, ' + this.username);
    }, 0);
  }
};
person.greet();

You have this code:

interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email?: string;
}

function printEmail(user: User) {
  console.log(user.email.toUpperCase());
}

What happens when you try to compile this with TypeScript's strict mode enabled?

Given this type narrowing code:

function describe(value: string | number) {
  if (typeof value === 'string') {
    return value.toUpperCase();
  }
  return value.toFixed(2);
}

Why does TypeScript allow calling both .toUpperCase() and .toFixed(2) without errors?

What will the following code print?

function outer() {
  let count = 0;
  return function increment() {
    count += 1;
    return count;
  };
}

const counter = outer();
console.log(counter());
console.log(counter());

